{
  "title": "CHAPTER 5 : GRAVITATION",
  "order": 5,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "cayhcul",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO GRAVITATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Gravitational force is a universal force acting between all objects with mass, calculated as F = G × (m₁m₂ / r²).",
        "Acceleration due to gravity (g = 9.8 m/s²) decreases with both height and depth, and varies across latitudes.",
        "Mass is the amount of matter in a body and remains constant everywhere, while Weight is the gravitational force acting on it (W = mg).",
        "According to Archimedes' Principle, a body immersed in fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced.",
        "Floatation depends on density: objects float if their density is less than the liquid density and sink if greater."
      ],
      "id": "cpy83iv"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Gravitational Constant (G)",
          "value": "6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Value of g on Earth",
          "value": "9.8 m/s²",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Pressure",
          "value": "Pascal (Pa)",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Universal Law Discovered By",
          "value": "Sir Isaac Newton",
